\title{A robotic arm}
\taxon{Example}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{Let’s look at a full production system. We imagine this system is controlling the actions of a robotic arm at a construction site, whose job it is to move bricks (stacked in a heap). We want these bricks to be ultimately laid out in weight order.}

\p{Our system starts with the production rules:}

##{\begin{aligned}
&\text{If } (\text{brick}, \text{position} : \text{heap}, \text{name} : x, \text{weight} : y) \\
&\qquad \wedge \neg(\text{brick}, \text{position} : \text{heap}, \text{weight} : \lbrace {>}\,y\rbrace ) \\
&\qquad \wedge \neg(\text{brick}, \text{position} : \text{hand}) \\
&\text{Then Modify } 1\; (\text{position} : \text{hand})
\end{aligned}}

##{\begin{aligned}
&\text{If } (\text{brick}, \text{position} : \text{hand}) \wedge (\text{current}, \text{position} : i) \\
&\text{Then Modify } 1\; (\text{position} : i) \\
&\qquad\; \; \, \text{Modify } 2\; (\text{position} : [i + 1])
\end{aligned}}

\p{For concreteness, perhaps we also begin with the working memory}

##{\begin{gathered}
(\text{current}, \text{position} : 1), \\
(\text{brick}, \text{position} : \text{heap}, \text{name} : A, \text{weight} : 10), \\
(\text{brick}, \text{position} : \text{heap}, \text{name} : B, \text{weight} : 30), \\
(\text{brick}, \text{position} : \text{heap}, \text{name} : C, \text{weight} : 20).
\end{gathered}}
